clean_summary:
I made a CLI with a TUI that I use every day. It features a test-time compute scaling feature-review loop and can use any agent harness as a backend, including Claude Code, Codex, or Cursor. It allows for rewinding, sending live guidance, worktrees, and more, all in a filesystem-first approach that doesn't require a tmux session to keep running.

The advantage of this setup over Claude Code is that it aims for high-quality code while ensuring it's bug-free by letting Claude implement and Codex review in a loop. I plan to add more features based on my needs, such as an orchestrator agent to manage tasks based on Linear tickets and autoresearch loops with appropriate evaluation or test-suite blackboxing. This all started because I was tired of doing the same tasks manually, and now it's easier to kick off runs, review, and iterate.

@casper_hansen_: "What would you do to get the code quality of Claude but the bug-free edge-case handling of GPT? In my case, I have an outer and inner loop, running with implementor -> review roles. Both inner and outer reviews have to pass before the loop exits. Very effective so far!"

This is quite interesting. I made the exact same recipe 8 months ago, but never expected someone to launch this commercially.

TLDR; you can get Fable performance with Opus using 2x Opus tokens.

@OpenRouter: "Introducing the Fusion API, the smartest compound model in the market. Fusion achieves Fable-level intelligence at half the price. How it works."

This should be talked more about. GLM finds that PPO is better than GRPO for agents that compact and therefore break the turn history.

@billxbf: "We therefore move from group-wise optimization to a critic-based PPO formulation that learns from individual rollouts, relying on a critic to estimate token-level advantages rather than group-relative comparisons. This single-rollout formulation fits compaction naturally, as it places no constraint on how many traces a prompt produces or on their relative lengths."

It is so true that PPO fits better in multi-trace credit assignment for agent RL. Still surprised how @Zai_org speeds on carrying this through at scale!

This uptick is the start of a new wave. glm-5.2 is a different kind of quality that signals the gap is closing between closed and open source models.

So the natural next step is full adoption, which means securing your compute and building your post-training stack.

@willccbb: "Something has definitely shifted in the past few weeks. Seeing a huge uptick in large enterprises wanting to secure compute and post-train their own models in house, frequently on top of glm-5.2. Everyone is starting to understand how open source wins."
